        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is polymeric sand better than regular sand for pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most concrete paver patios, walkways and driveways, yes. It keeps joints full, resists weeds and ants, and rarely washes out when installed properly. Regular sand is cheaper and easier to install but needs topping up and weeding. Regular sand is the better choice for bases that move, temporary surfaces, or where you cannot meet the dry-weather installation conditions polymeric sand needs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What kind of regular sand is best for paver joints?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use an angular sand that packs tightly, such as kiln-dried silica paver joint sand or dry concrete sand. Kiln-dried sand flows into narrow joints most easily. Avoid play sand and mason sand, which are fine and rounded and wash out quickly. Whatever you choose, it must be dry to sweep into narrow joints properly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I mix polymeric sand with regular sand?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not as a cost-saving blend. Diluting polymeric sand with plain sand reduces the binder content and weakens the joint, and manufacturers do not recommend it. Use polymeric sand straight from the bag. If budget is a concern, use regular sand on less important areas and polymeric sand where weeds and washout bother you most."
          }
