          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a concrete paver patio, walkway or driveway on a sound base, polymeric sand is the better choice. It costs more up front and takes a careful weekend to install, but it ends the cycle of washed-out joints, weeds and ant hills that comes with plain sand.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Regular sand still makes sense for bases that move, temporary or casual surfaces, and situations where you cannot meet the dry-weather installation polymeric sand demands. If you are switching, clear the old sand fully, let the joints dry, and follow the installation steps exactly.
          </p>
